  <h2>Back it up</h2>
  <p>
    A backup runs every night at 03:17 UTC into <code>/var/backups/zafe-relay/testnet</code>,
    kept 14 days. Copy that folder off the machine too. Take one by hand with:
  </p>
  <pre><code>sudo docker compose exec backup-testnet sh /backup.sh once</code></pre>
  <p>
    A lost or old database doesn’t lose funds or vaults: every phone keeps the history,
    and a member taps <strong>Restore vault on the server</strong> on the Status screen. Restore
    the newest backup anyway; the steps are in the
    <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/infra/relay/README.md#backups`}>relay README</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Update it</h2>
  <pre><code>sudo docker compose exec backup-testnet sh /backup.sh once
sudo docker compose pull &amp;&amp; sudo docker compose up -d</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>main</code> is the image Zafe’s test server runs after every change. To choose when you
    update, use a <code>sha-&lt;commit&gt;</code> tag instead. An outdated server shows in the
    app as “Needs an update”. To check where an image came from:
  </p>
  <pre><code>gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/zafe-cash/zafe-relay:main -R zafe-cash/zafe</code></pre>

  <h2>Defaults</h2>
  <p>Add any of these to <code>secrets.env</code> to change them; <code>0</code> turns one off.</p>
  <dl class="doc-vars">
    <div><dt><code>ZAFE_RELAY_KEY_RATE</code> · <code>ZAFE_RELAY_IP_RATE</code></dt><dd>Requests per minute: 300 per member key, 1,200 per IP address. Over them the server answers “busy”. <code>ZAFE_RELAY_LIMITS=off</code> turns all rate limits off.</dd></div>
    <div><dt><code>ZAFE_RELAY_CREATES_PER_DAY</code> · <code>ZAFE_RELAY_MAX_VAULTS_PER_KEY</code></dt><dd>New vaults: 20 a day per IP address, 8 per key.</dd></div>
    <div><dt><code>ZAFE_RELAY_MAX_INBOX</code> · <code>ZAFE_RELAY_MAX_DELIVERY_MB</code> · <code>ZAFE_RELAY_MAX_LOG_MB</code></dt><dd>Per vault: 10,000 waiting messages per member, 256 MiB of waiting messages, 512 MiB of history. <code>ZAFE_RELAY_QUOTAS=off</code> turns these off.</dd></div>
    <div><dt><code>ZAFE_RELAY_IDLE_DAYS</code> · <code>ZAFE_RELAY_UNUSED_DAYS</code></dt><dd>A vault is deleted after 365 days with no member request, or 30 days after it was created if no member but its creator ever connected. <code>ZAFE_RELAY_EXPIRY=off</code> keeps everything.</dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p>Messages waiting for a member are deleted once picked up, and after 30 days in any case.</p>

  <h2>Good to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No notifications: they need Zafe’s notification key, so only Zafe’s servers send them. Phones check in the background and when Zafe opens.</li>
    <li>Every member must reach it over HTTPS, with a certificate phones trust. Members who use Tor can’t reach a private network address.</li>
    <li>You see what any server sees: who talks to which vault, when, and from which IP address. The Caddy setup keeps no access log; keep it that way.</li>
    <li>Run one instance per database file. Never two containers on the same data, and no scaling out.</li>
    <li>Want it in the app’s server list? Open a pull request adding it to <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/app/lib/src/core/config/relay_presets.dart`}><code>relay_presets.dart</code></a>, with your name as its operator.</li>
    <li>Own reverse proxy instead of Caddy? Let responses take 40 seconds or more (the app holds requests open for 25), and set <code>ZAFE_RELAY_CLIENT_IP_HEADER</code> to a header your proxy overwrites.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If it goes wrong</h2>
  <dl class="doc-fixes">
    <div><dt>The app says “Not a Zafe server.” or “Couldn’t connect.”</dt><dd>Open <code>https://relay.example.org/health</code> in a browser: it should say <code>ok</code>. If the certificate fails, check that DNS points straight at the server, then <code>sudo docker compose logs caddy</code>.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>“It runs an older Zafe server and can’t hold new vaults.”</dt><dd>Pull the newest image (above).</dd></div>
    <div><dt>“This server is private: it needs its create token.”</dt><dd>Send the member <code>relay.example.org#&lt;token&gt;</code>.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</Doc>
